WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.990
Welcome to Grade 4 English, Unit 5: Action and Movement.

2
00:00:04.070 --> 00:00:11.547
Unit 5 is about action and movement: how people and
animals move, how we describe fast and slow actions,

3
00:00:11.627 --> 00:00:16.489
and how we make good choices when
we feel excited or under pressure.

4
00:00:16.569 --> 00:00:23.392
You will read about a runner who learns to
accelerate, animals that gallop, squeeze and spiral,

5
00:00:23.472 --> 00:00:26.372
and three children who rescue a lost dog.

6
00:00:26.452 --> 00:00:31.386
You will meet strong verbs such as
squeeze, peek, proceed and defend,

7
00:00:31.466 --> 00:00:37.708
and you will learn to say exactly how an
action happens — quickly, slowly or carefully.

8
00:00:37.788 --> 00:00:42.577
Everything leads to this unit’s
adventure story, “The Spiral Cave”.

9
00:00:42.657 --> 00:00:48.682
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

10
00:00:48.762 --> 00:00:56.797
By the end of this unit, you will be able to use strong
action verbs to describe how people and animals move.

11
00:00:56.877 --> 00:01:04.541
You will also tell a story about the past using the
past simple tense (played, galloped, rescued, went).

12
00:01:04.621 --> 00:01:10.721
You will also describe actions with adverbs
of manner (quickly, slowly, carefully).

13
00:01:10.801 --> 00:01:13.476
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:13.556 --> 00:01:19.515
Your vocabulary work begins with these words:
gallop, accelerate, proceed, spiral, rate, peek.

15
00:01:19.595 --> 00:01:24.718
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:24.798 --> 00:01:28.765
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:28.845 --> 00:01:33.881
The reading sequence includes The Race
at the Village Field and How Animals Move.

18
00:01:33.961 --> 00:01:34.765
Read actively.

19
00:01:34.845 --> 00:01:39.123
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:39.203 --> 00:01:43.229
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:43.309 --> 00:01:46.453
The first language focus is Past Simple Tense.

22
00:01:46.533 --> 00:01:52.130
The past simple tense talks about actions
that already happened and are finished.

23
00:01:52.210 --> 00:01:57.948
It lets us tell stories about the past,
such as what we did yesterday or last week,

24
00:01:58.028 --> 00:02:03.275
as in “She rescued the bird yesterday”
and “They galloped across the field”.

25
00:02:03.355 --> 00:02:10.915
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

26
00:02:10.995 --> 00:02:14.984
In speaking, you will complete Speaking 1: Describe an Action.

27
00:02:15.064 --> 00:02:19.380
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

28
00:02:19.460 --> 00:02:22.727
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

29
00:02:22.807 --> 00:02:26.205
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

30
00:02:26.285 --> 00:02:27.851
You are ready to begin.

31
00:02:27.931 --> 00:02:34.934
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

32
00:02:35.014 --> 00:02:38.368
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

33
00:02:38.448 --> 00:02:46.454
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
